Root cause of flaky tests: the fake clock wasn't paused before the page loaded. Timers, Date.now(), and animations that start during load ran on real time, so pass/fail depended on machine speed.

Fix: install/pause the fake clock BEFORE navigating. Playwright example:
  await page.clock.install({ time: new Date('2024-01-01T00:00:00') });
  await page.clock.pauseAt(new Date('2024-01-01T00:00:00'));
  await page.goto(url);
  await page.clock.runFor(1000); // advance time deliberately

Related gotchas: set up the clock in the context that actually loads the page (re-navigations/new pages); workers/iframes may not share the fake clock; avoid real-time waits like waitForTimeout and advance via the clock API instead.

Root cause of flaky tests: the fake clock wasn't installed/paused before the page loaded, so app startup code captured real Date.now() values and scheduled real timers.

Fix (Playwright): set up the clock BEFORE navigating.
  await page.clock.install({ time: new Date('2024-01-01T08:00:00') });
  await page.clock.pauseAt(new Date('2024-01-01T10:00:00'));
  await page.goto('/your-page');
  await page.clock.runFor('02:00'); // advance time deliberately

Tips:
- Put clock setup in a shared beforeEach/fixture so no test forgets it.
- Advance time with runFor/fastForward, not real waits like waitForTimeout.
- Use setFixedTime if you only need Date frozen but want timers to run normally.

Root cause of flaky tests: the fake clock wasn't installed/paused before the page loaded, so timers, Date.now() calls, and animations set up during load ran on real time, which made results depend on load speed.

Fix (Playwright): install the clock before navigating, then advance time explicitly.

await page.clock.install({ time: new Date('2026-01-01T00:00:00') });
await page.clock.pauseAt(new Date('2026-01-01T00:00:00')); // optional, freeze time
await page.goto(url);
await page.clock.runFor(1000);

Related things to watch for:
- Service workers and iframes may not use the faked clock.
- Reusing a page across tests can carry clock state between tests.
- fastForward fires each due timer at most once; runFor runs every timer in the window, so intervals can fire many times.

